tching up”. Not only did Hong Kong’s manufacturing firms lack the motivation to seek product innovations, the buyer-driven and trade-led production governance structure also provided much organizational inertia for small and medium-sized firms to expand into large firms. Compared to other major East Asian competitors, such as Japan, Taiwan, Singapore and South Korea, Hong Kong’s many signature industries in the 1960s and 1970s (e.g. toys, garments, electronics, plastics industries) gradually lost their competitiveness in the 1980s. If laissezfaire may not be the primary contributory factor for Hong Kong’s industrial takeoff, it definitely was one of the key backgrounds behind the
